# vllm_openai_xpu/.env.example — copy to `.env` (auto-read by Compose, gitignored).
#
# This engine alternates between TWO validated models. Uncomment EXACTLY ONE of
# the two blocks below, then apply it:
#
#   docker compose -f vllm_openai_xpu/compose.yaml up -d
#
# A plain `up -d` is enough: Compose hashes each container's settings with the
# values from this file filled in, and recreates it when the hash changes.
# `docker compose restart` does NOT pick up an edit.
# ./README.md "Applying a .env change"
#
# !! PICK THE LINES FOR YOUR CARD. Each block has one KV line per card (Arc Pro
#    B60 = 24 GB, Arc Pro B70 = 32 GB); keep exactly one active. The values are
#    explained in ../INTEL_ARC_B60.md and ../INTEL_ARC_B70.md.
#
# !! SWITCH A WHOLE BLOCK AT ONCE. Three variables are tied to the model and
#    fail in three different ways if carried across:
#      - VLLM_KV_CACHE_MEMORY is ABSOLUTE bytes and OOMs rather than shrinking.
#        On a B60, gemma-4's weights (15.76 GiB) + gpt-oss's 8.0 GiB pin
#        exceed the 22.33 GiB free => boot OOM. The reverse boots but strands
#        ~4 GiB and halves the pool.
#      - VLLM_EAGER_FLAG: on a B60, --no-enforce-eager with gpt-oss's 8.0 GiB
#        pin STARVES the KV pool and the boot dies. It is the PIN that decides
#        this, not the context size: gemma-4 runs compiled at 131072 fine.
#      - VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN: both blocks use 131072 today, but keep it in each
#        block so the two are free to diverge.
#    A mismatched reasoning/tool parser does NOT crash; it quietly yields an
#    empty reasoning field or no tool_calls. Easy to miss.
#    Leaving a block fully commented is always safe: every variable falls back
#    to the compose default, which is the gpt-oss-20b block's B60 values.
#
# !! SINGLE GPU — `down` the other engine folder first (vllm_xpu/ or scaler/).
#
# To run the OTHER block temporarily WITHOUT editing this file, use the shell
# override block in ./README.md ("Switching models -> Temporary"). Shell vars
# beat .env, but ONLY for the names you pass — every variable the block sets
# must be overridden, including VLLM_EAGER_FLAG and VLLM_KV_CACHE_MEMORY.

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# BLOCK A — gpt-oss-20b   (compose default)
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 12.87 GiB of weights on either card. Measured on the B60: 83.2 tok/s
# (bench.sh chunk count), 338,928-token KV pool = 2.59x concurrency @128k.
# NOT MEASURED ON THE B70 YET, so it has no B70 value of its own. The B60 value
# should boot there (it is smaller than the room available) but leaves memory
# unused. To find the B70 value, boot once with the --kv-cache-memory-bytes
# line commented out in compose.yaml and read the advised value from the log.
# Reasoning is ALWAYS on and has no off switch, so VLLM_DEFAULT_CHAT_TEMPLATE_-
# KWARGS is left unset here (an unknown template kwarg is verified harmless).
# EAGER IS REQUIRED with the B60's 8.0 GiB pin — do not add VLLM_EAGER_FLAG.
# gpt-oss's prefill stays LINEAR at 131,072, whereas gemma-4 on Triton would
# need ~92 min to cold-fill that much (B60). With Intel attention (below),
# gemma-4 read 129,331 tokens in 51.7 s (B70), but without images.
#
#VLLM_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-20b
#VLLM_SERVED_MODEL_NAME=gpt-oss-20b
#VLLM_REASONING_PARSER=openai_gptoss
#VLLM_TOOL_CALL_PARSER=openai
#VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN=131072
# Arc Pro B60: 8.0 GiB -> 338,928 tokens (2.59x)
#VLLM_KV_CACHE_MEMORY=8603448832
# Arc Pro B70: not measured yet

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# BLOCK B — gemma-4-26B-A4B-it, offline int4  (newer model, vision-capable)
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The checkpoint and the KV value go together per card. Each pair below is the
# one boot-tested on that card at 131,072. On the B70 adeepv is ~8% faster than
# reinforce20001 (same-day comparison) and ~1.1 GiB lighter. On the B60 both
# ran at ~56 tok/s compiled, in different sessions. Both are int4 group-32
# builds of Google's own QAT weights.
#
# CONTEXT IS NOT VRAM-LIMITED. 25 of gemma-4's 30 layers are sliding-attention
# (window 1024) and their KV does NOT scale with context; only the 5
# full-attention layers do, at 20 KiB/token. MEASURED on the B60 at the SAME
# 4.25 GiB pin: 32768 -> 2.39x | 65536 -> 1.77x | 131072 -> 1.16x, with
# weights, pool, decode and TTFT all unchanged. Ceiling there is 162,496; on
# the B70 the checkpoint's own 262,144 limit comes first. Do NOT raise
# --max-num-batched-tokens: it feeds max_in_flight_tokens, inflates the SLIDING
# reservation, and at 8192 it collapses the B60 ceiling to 48,576.
# ./README.md "Context is nearly free"
#
# THE CAP IS A GUARDRAIL, not a memory limit and NOT a speed setting. Decode
# and TTFT are identical at 32k / 64k / 128k (measured). What the cap DOES
# control is the LONGEST PREFILL A CLIENT CAN TRIGGER. On Triton (the default)
# prefill is quadratic: on the B60 ~4.6 min at 32k, ~22 min at 64k, ~92 min at
# 128k (fitted, T ~= 26.3 s * (n/9643)^2.048, measured up to 64,708 tokens);
# the B70 is ~1.4-1.5x faster on the same curve. With Intel attention (below)
# a 129,331-token prompt took 51.7 s on the B70. If you stay on Triton and
# clients can send huge cold prompts, a lower cap makes them fail fast with a
# 400 instead. ./README.md "The cap is a guardrail"
#
# PREFIX CACHING makes a repeated prompt cheap. On the B60 (Triton), sending
# the same prompt twice: 9,643 tok 26.3 s -> 0.31 s (84x), 31,957 tok
# 305.3 s -> 0.63 s (481x). The cache lives in VRAM and does NOT survive a
# container recreate.
#
# CHECKPOINT CONSTRAINT: must be int4-symmetric with group_size 32 or
# channelwise. The XPU expert kernel (XPUExpertsWNA16) accepts only those two
# schemes, so the smaller and far more popular group-64 builds are REJECTED at
# load. Check quantization_config.config_groups.*.weights.group_size before
# trying another.
#
VLLM_SERVED_MODEL_NAME=gemma-4-26b-a4b
VLLM_REASONING_PARSER=gemma4
VLLM_TOOL_CALL_PARSER=gemma4
VLLM_MAX_MODEL_LEN=131072
#
# Arc Pro B70: adeepv, 10.5 GiB -> 376,999 tokens (2.88x) on Triton,
# 394,408 (3.01x) with Intel attention
VLLM_MODEL=adeepv/gemma-4-26B-A4B-it-W4A16-vLLM
VLLM_KV_CACHE_MEMORY=11274289152
#
# Arc Pro B60: reinforce20001, 4.25 GiB -> 152,592 tokens (1.16x)
#VLLM_MODEL=reinforce20001/gemma4-26b-a4b-it-qat-w4a16-ct
#VLLM_KV_CACHE_MEMORY=4563402752

# Compiled mode instead of eager, measured faster: on the B60, 0.30.0 decoded
# at 56.46 tok/s compiled against 53.25 eager. Verified with the pool intact at
# 32768, 65536 AND 131072 on the B60's 4.25 GiB pin, and it is what the B70
# runs at 10.5 GiB, because the compile buffers scale with
# --max-num-batched-tokens (2496), NOT context.
# Costs ~34 s of torch.compile on a cold cache (the SYCL cache must stay off)
# and does NOT help prefill.
# Passed as the WHOLE flag: argparse builds this as a BooleanOptionalAction, so
# `--enforce-eager=False` does not parse — it must be `--no-enforce-eager`.
# !! DO NOT carry this into Block A on a B60: eager is REQUIRED with gpt-oss's
# 8.0 GiB pin there. With that pin the compile buffers, which
# gpu-memory-utilization does not cap, starve the KV pool and the boot dies.
VLLM_EAGER_FLAG=--no-enforce-eager

# gemma-4's reasoning is OPT-IN — its chat template defaults enable_thinking to
# false, unlike gpt-oss which always reasons. This turns it on server-side so
# clients need no special field. VERIFIED:
#   unset/null   -> ./smoke.sh reasoning check FAILS, reasoning len 0
#   set as below -> ./smoke.sh PASSES with no client flag
# A request still overrides it BOTH ways (also verified):
#   "chat_template_kwargs": {"enable_thinking": false}   -> suppresses it
# It does NOT cost decode speed (measured by usage.completion_tokens —
# bench.sh's lower figure with reasoning on is an artifact of counting SSE
# chunks). Its real cost is BUDGET: reasoning consumes max_tokens first, so at
# 400 you get a trace and no answer. Allow 1500+.
# Comment out to go back to per-request opt-in.
#VLLM_DEFAULT_CHAT_TEMPLATE_KWARGS={"enable_thinking":true}

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# After any switch
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The served model name changes with the block, so DOWNSTREAM BREAKS until it is
# repointed: an AI gateway (LiteLLM, Bifrost — any gateway) maps a fixed model
# id and will 404 against the other block. Repoint the consumer's model mapping.
# Both blocks run at 131,072 today, but the models cost very differently on a
# big cold prompt: do NOT serve one model under the other's name. Keep names
# truthful, one per model.
#
# Verify. Both scripts auto-detect the served model when MODEL is unset, print
# the real checkpoint behind the label, and take a tri-state THINKING
# (auto = report what the DEPLOYED default does):
#   ./smoke.sh              # ALL PASS on both blocks as configured above
#   THINKING=1 ./smoke.sh   # force reasoning on  -> expects a trace
#   THINKING=0 ./smoke.sh   # force reasoning off -> expects it SUPPRESSED
#   ./bench.sh 400          # speed; counts SSE chunks, see ./README.md
#   ./bench.sh 1500         # with reasoning on, needs the larger budget

# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Engine knobs — shared by both blocks, rarely changed
# ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# MUST stay 0 while VLLM_USE_V2_MODEL_RUNNER=1: 1 segfaults at boot inside the
# SYCL persistent code-cache read. Costs a kernel rebuild each boot (~70 s).
#SYCL_CACHE_PERSISTENT=0

# Upstream default from 0.29.0 on; measured faster and leaner here. 0 pins V1.
# Integer only — a blank value crashes at boot.
#VLLM_USE_V2_MODEL_RUNNER=1

# Compiled vs eager. Default --enforce-eager is required by Block A's B60 pin;
# --no-enforce-eager is safe for Block B at any context up to 131,072
# (boot-tested), because compile buffers scale with max-num-batched-tokens.
#VLLM_EAGER_FLAG=--enforce-eager

# XPU graph. Only does anything in COMPILED mode — a genuine no-op under eager.
# On the B60 it captured graphs but cost 1.54 GiB, dropping the pool 78,433 ->
# 46,138 tokens at 32k for +0.5%. On the B70 the GPU is already 99-100% busy
# during decode, so there is nothing for it to win. Not worth it on either card.
#VLLM_XPU_ENABLE_XPU_GRAPH=0

# Attention backend: auto (default) or FLASH_ATTN (fast, no images)
#VLLM_ATTN_BACKEND=FLASH_ATTN

# Required with FLASH_ATTN above; disables image input
#VLLM_TEXT_ONLY_FLAG=--language-model-only

# Empty = anonymous. Only needed for gated repos; neither block above is gated.
#HF_TOKEN=
